data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","sequence_number":2168,"output_index":1,"item":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_09e7677d2c9d8e14016adb2f3e524c81919dac45e33c9b7888","call_id":"call_d932b97d4fe346e9b19d90444c9007bc","name":"submit_artifact","arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"COMPLETED\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"For every prime p, field K of characteristic p, integer lambda with 0 <= lambda < p, and arbitrary admissible lattice M in V(lambda), the module M tensor_Z K is nonzero and irreducible under L(K).\\\",\\\"obstacle_assessment\\\":{\\\"classification\\\":\\\"ROUTINE_COMPLETION_NOT_SUBSTANTIVE_GAP\\\",\\\"reasoning\\\":\\\"The reported issue concerns finite generation and rank of the coordinate module J. Both follow from the matrix-unit decomposition and elementary localization/PID arguments; no unsupported implication remains. In particular, the common coordinate module is a finitely generated nonzero submodule of C, its rank follows by tensoring the finite direct-sum decomposition with Q, and it is free rank one because every finitely generated fractional ideal of Z_(p) is principal. The resulting generator gives an explicit reduced basis with the standard action. This preserves arbitrary M and does not assume distinct reduced h-weights.\\\"},\\\"complete_proof\\\":\\\"Fix p,K,lambda,M satisfying the stated hypotheses, and put n=lambda+1 and R=Z_(p)={a/b in Q : p does not divide b}. Let v be a nonzero highest-weight vector in V(lambda) and put e_i=y^i v/i! for 0<=i<=lambda. These form a C-basis. Put X_r=x^r/r! and Y_r=y^r/r!. Repeated use of [h,x]=2x, [h,y]=-2y, [x,y]=h and xv=0, hv=lambda v gives, for 0<=i<=lambda and r>=0, X_r e_i=binom(lambda-i+r,r)e_(i-r), and Y_r e_i=binom(i+r,r)e_(i+r), interpreting e_k as zero when k is outside [0,lambda]. For example, x e_i=(lambda-i+1)e_(i-1) and y e_i=(i+1)e_(i+1); iterating these formulas and dividing by r! gives the displayed binomial coefficients. Thus all divided-power operators have integral matrices in this basis.\\n\\nLet M_R=R tensor_Z M, embedded in V(lambda) using the given inclusion M subset V(lambda), and let A_R be the R-subalgebra of End_C(V(lambda)) generated by the actions of the divided powers. Since M is admissible, each divided power preserves M, and therefore every element of A_R preserves M_R.\\n\\nFor 0<=a,b<=lambda let E_ab be the operator that sends e_b to e_a and sends all other basis vectors to zero. The formulas give Y_lambda X_lambda=E_lambda,lambda: X_lambda vanishes on e_i for i<lambda, sends e_lambda to e_0, and Y_lambda sends e_0 to e_lambda. They also give X_(lambda-i) E_lambda,lambda=E_i,lambda. Finally, E_lambda,lambda Y_(lambda-j)=binom(lambda,j)E_lambda,j. Because 0<=lambda<p, neither lambda! nor j!(lambda-j)! is divisible by p, so binom(lambda,j) is a unit in R. Consequently E_lambda,j belongs to A_R (multiply the preceding operator by this unit's inverse), and E_i,j=E_i,lambda E_lambda,j belongs to A_R for every i,j. This also works for lambda=0: the sole E_00 is the identity. Thus every E_i,j preserves M_R.\\n\\nFor each i define J_i={c in C : c e_i belongs to M_R}. The R-module M_R is free of rank n, since M is free of rank n, so it is finitely generated. E_ii(M_R)=J_i e_i; hence J_i e_i, and therefore J_i, is a finitely generated R-module. It is nonzero: M_R spans V(lambda) over C because an R-basis of R tensor M is also a C-basis after extending scalars, and applying the nonzero projection E_ii to that span gives C e_i. If J_i were zero, E_ii would vanish on M_R and hence on its C-span, contradiction.\\n\\nIf c belongs to J_j, then c e_j belongs to M_R, and E_ij(c e_j)=c e_i belongs to M_R, so c belongs to J_i. Reversing i and j proves J_i=J_j. Write J for their common value. For any m in M_R, its i-th coordinate component is E_ii m and lies in M_R, so it has the form c_i e_i with c_i in J. Hence M_R is contained in the direct sum of the J e_i. Conversely c in J means c e_i in M_R for every i, so that direct sum is contained in M_R. Since the e_i are C-linearly independent, the sum is direct, and M_R=direct_sum_{i=0}^{lambda} J e_i.\\n\\nHere are the needed elementary module details. Let Q be the fraction field of R. The inclusion J subset C makes J torsion-free over R. It is nonzero and finitely generated. Localizing the direct-sum equality at the nonzero elements of R gives Q tensor_R M_R = direct_sum_i (Q tensor_R J)e_i. The left side has Q-dimension n because M_R is free of rank n. The right side has dimension n times dim_Q(Q tensor_R J); thus dim_Q(Q tensor_R J)=1. Choose a Q-linear isomorphism Q tensor_R J -> Q. Its image I of J is a nonzero finitely generated R-submodule of Q. Choose a common nonzero denominator d in R for a finite set of generators of I; then dI is a nonzero finitely generated ideal of R. Every nonzero element q of Q can be written p^k u with k an integer and u a unit in R: write q=p^k a/b with p not dividing a,b. For a nonzero finitely generated ideal B of R, take finite generators, and let k be the smallest exponent of p among their nonzero elements. Every generator lies in p^k R, while a generator with exponent k is p^k times a unit, so B=p^k R. Apply this to dI. It follows that I is principal, and the isomorphism identifies J with a free rank-one R-module. In particular choose a generator a of J; then M_R has R-basis a e_0,...,a e_lambda. No restriction on the original lattice M has been made.\\n\\nThe action of x,y,h on that basis is obtained directly from the divided-power formulas at r=1 and h e_i=(lambda-2i)e_i: x(ae_i)=(lambda-i+1)ae_(i-1), y(ae_i)=(i+1)ae_(i+1), and h(ae_i)=(lambda-2i)ae_i, with out-of-range terms zero. Hence reduction modulo p gives the standard reduced module on the images of e_0,...,e_lambda; the factor a merely gives the chosen basis. More explicitly, localization then reduction identifies M_R/pM_R with M tensor_Z F_p: if m_0,...,m_lambda is any Z-basis of M, both have the corresponding basis reduced modulo p, and the action matrices are the reductions of the same integral matrices. The preceding R-basis identifies this reduced module with the standard module. Since K has characteristic p, its prime subfield is F_p; extending scalars along F_p subset K identifies M tensor_Z K with the standard module over K, compatibly with x,y,h.\\n\\nIt remains to prove irreducibility of that standard module. For 0<=r<=lambda<p, r! is nonzero in F_p and therefore invertible. Thus the operators X_r and Y_r on the reduced module equal (1/r!)x^r and (1/r!)y^r, respectively, and are generated by compositions and scalar multiples of x and y. The matrix-unit calculations above remain valid after reduction: each binom(lambda,j) remains nonzero modulo p and invertible, so every E_i,j is a composition and scalar multiple of such operators, and hence preserves any K-subspace invariant under x,y,h. Let W be such a subspace. If W is nonzero, choose w=sum_i c_i e_i in W with some c_i nonzero. For each j, E_ji w=c_i e_j belongs to W. Since c_i is invertible in K, e_j belongs to W for every j; these vectors are a basis, so W is the whole module. The module is nonzero since its dimension is lambda+1>=1. When lambda=0 the same argument says the one-dimensional module has only zero and itself as subspaces. Nothing in the argument requires p>2 or distinct h-eigenvalues. This proves the target.\\\",\\\"source_obligations\\\":\\\"No literature theorem is imported in this completion. The highest-weight basis and divided-power formulas are derived in the proof from the stated sl2 relations and highest-weight equations by iteration. The matrix-unit identities are explicit calculations. Finite generation, localization, dimension, the principal-ideal argument for Z_(p), reduction, and extension of scalars are given explicitly. The starting facts that V(lambda) has the stated highest-weight vector/basis dimension, that M is free of rank lambda+1 and spans V(lambda), and that admissibility means U_Z M subset M are supplied in source-context.md and the frozen hypotheses/context; they are used as stated, not as a claim about the desired modular irreducibility.\\\",\\\"edge_cases\\\":\\\"lambda=0: n=1, the matrix-unit construction gives E_00=identity and the one-dimensional module is nonzero and irreducible. p=2 is included; lambda<p then forces lambda=0 or 1, the binomial coefficients used are units, and no division by 2 or separation of h-weights is used. Arbitrary admissible lattices are covered by the coordinate-module argument; no minimal-lattice assumption is made. Any field K of characteristic p is handled by extension from its prime subfield F_p.\\\"}\"}","status":"completed"}}

